BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:maptimeDavis: Spatial SQL
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260529T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260529T120000
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52497914121538
DESCRIPTION:Description: This workshop is intended to give participants an
  introduction to working with spatial data using SQL -- a database queryin
 g language. We will work with a graphical user interface (GUI) and explore
  some examples of common analysis processes as well as present participant
 s with resources for continued learning. This workshop will give participa
 nts a solid foundation on which to build further learning. By the end of t
 his workshop\, participants will be able to import data into a spatialite 
 database\, write queries to answer questions about spatial data\, understa
 nd the difference between attribute queries and geometry queries\, view sp
 atial tables and views in QGIS\, and use terminology related to spatial da
 tabases to facilitate future learning.\n\n \n\nConcepts Covered: Databases
 \, SQL\, Spatial Analysis\, Data Management\n\n \n\nSpeakers: Alex Mandel\
 , Michele Tobias\, Naomi Kalman\, Holden Tal\n\n \n\nThis workshop will be
  taught by a team of instructors who use spatial SQL in their work and res
 earch.\n\n \n\nPrerequisites: No prior experience is required\, but famili
 arity with spatial data formats (raster and vector) is recommended.\n\n \n
 \nSoftware Required: QGIS: available for all major computer operating syst
 ems at qgis.org\n\n \n\nPlease RSVP.
LOCATION:DataLab\, 360 Shields Library
URL:https://events.library.ucdavis.edu/event/maptimedavis-spatial-sql
X-SOURCE:UC Davis Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI Literacy & Logic Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T163000
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52340792789232
DESCRIPTION:A free 1-day intensive workshop for College of Letters & Scien
 ce majors covering foundational principles and skills for using Artificial
  Intelligence (AI).\n\n \n\nThe landscape of the academic and professional
  world is shifting. Whether you are an aspiring historian\, a budding chem
 ist\, or a future sociologist\, AI is no longer a "tech" skill—it’s a 
 foundational literacy.\n\n \n\nDeveloped through a collaboration between t
 he UC Davis College of Letters & Science and DataLab\, this 1-day intensiv
 e workshop is designed to unveil Artificial Intelligence. We’ve stripped
  away the dense math and complex coding to give you a conceptual\, high-le
 vel understanding of how artificial intelligence systems work\, why they m
 atter\, and how to make them work for you.\n\n \n\nWhy Apply to Attend?\n\
 nIn a single day\, you will move from being a passive AI user to a critica
 l\, strategic collaborator. This training is specifically curated for L&S 
 students to ensure you graduate with the competitive edge needed for the r
 apidly evolving\, AI-driven workplace. According to a LinkedIn survey repo
 rt\, only about a third (38%) of U.S. executives say they're currently hel
 ping their employees become AI-literate. Meanwhile\, findings from a 2025 
 Businesswire study found that 79% of workers feel they must learn new skil
 ls and that AI literacy is the foundation for long-term competitiveness. S
 tudents who attend this all-day workshop and complete required pre- and po
 st-workshop activities will gain foundational and applied skills in AI\, a
 nd receive a certificate of completion.\n\n \n\nNo technical background? N
 o problem. This workshop has no prerequisites. It is designed for thinkers
 \, writers\, and researchers across all liberal arts\, social sciences and
  STEM disciplines.\n\n \n\nThe curriculum is divided into four deep-dive m
 odules:\n\nHow AI Thinks\nUnderstand the "brain" of the machine. We’ll e
 xplore the theories of language and cognition that allow Large Language Mo
 dels (LLMs) to function\, and provide a framework for evaluating their pot
 ential and limits.\n\nHow to Use AI Responsibly\nAI isn't a magic wand—i
 t’s a tool with a history and consequences. We discuss the environmental
  and societal impacts\, how to ensure the veracity of AI results\, and why
  reproducibility is the cornerstone of ethical tech.\n\nHow to Talk with A
 I\nMaster the art and science of prompt engineering. Learn what happens be
 hind the scenes when you send a prompt. Develop sophisticated strategies t
 o get the exact results you need.\n\nHow AI Talks with You\nA look at the 
 future of AI implementation. From simple chatbots to complex autonomous "A
 gents\," learn how AI is being embedded into systems and how to evaluate i
 ts activity.\n\n \n\nBy the end of this one-day workshop\, you will be abl
 e to:\n\nDefine "AI" and distinguish it from traditional statistics and da
 ta science.Identify whether a real-world problem is suitable for an AI sol
 ution.Develop advanced prompting strategies that produce reliable\, useful
  results for your research and career needs.Critically interrogate your ow
 n engagement with AI and its impact on our society and environment.Practic
 e and grow your confidence in using AI in the rapidly evolving\, AI-driven
  workplace. \n\nWho Can Apply?\n\nAll UC Davis undergraduate students with
  majors in the College of Letters & Science are eligible to apply. Registr
 ation priority is given to  Seniors (4th years) and students graduating in
  Spring or Summer 2026.  Other students in the College of Letters & Scienc
 e are encouraged to register and will be admitted as space permits. Accept
 ed learners are expected to attend all modules on their date of instructio
 n.\n\n \n\nApply Now to Participate\n\nThis intensive\, one-day workshop i
 s offered five times during Spring Quarter 2026. Students should apply for
  only one date. Space is limited so don't wait to apply!\n\nRegistering fo
 r a ticket means you are applying to attend. This does not guarantee your 
 participation. Accepted students will receive a confirmation email with th
 e workshop location.Time: 9:00 AM – 4:30 PM with a break for a provided 
 box lunch. Students must plan to attend all modules\, including the networ
 king lunch.Location: In person only. The classroom location will be emaile
 d to accepted students in advance of the workshop.Cost: This workshop is f
 ree for undergraduate students and supported by the UC Davis College of Le
 tters & Science. \n\nQuestions?\n\nReach out to Mark Foncannon at mjfoncan
 non@ucdavis.edu.
URL:https://events.library.ucdavis.edu/event/ai-literacy-logic-spring-2026
X-SOURCE:UC Davis Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Collaborative Data Science Office Hours (virtual)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260601T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260601T140000
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_50756015400073
DESCRIPTION:DataLab and colleagues from research computing and statistical
  service units across UC Davis offer weekly free virtual office hours duri
 ng which UC Davis researchers can obtain help and advice on working with t
 heir data. During these short\, just-in-time sessions we meet with faculty
 \, students\, postdoctoral scholars\, and research staff from all domains 
 across the University to discuss their research challenges.\n\nRegister in
  advance for the Zoom link. This helps us connect you to the right expert 
 for your question.\n\nIn addition to DataLab\, the collaborative data scie
 nce office hours are supported by:\n\nBiostatistics (Clinical and Translat
 ional Science Center\, CTSC)Computational Research Data Service (College o
 f Letters & Science)High Performance Computing (HPC) CoreResearcher Servic
 es (UC Davis Library)Stat Lab (Department of Statistics)Common office hour
 s help topics include:\n\nOpen Source Programming (R\, Python\, Julia\, et
 c.)Reproducible research computing and data scienceResponsible and ethical
  data scienceData gathering\, organization\, databases\, and experimental 
 designData analysis\, statistics\, and explorationData visualizationMachin
 e learning and neural networksText mining and natural language processingG
 eospatial sciencesRemote and high performance computingComputational pedag
 ogy… and more!Please note that drop-in office hours are not offered duri
 ng University holidays\, academic breaks and finals week. Summer schedule 
 varies based on availability.\n\nNote: Office hours are intended for short
  "just in time" research support. If you require extensive assistance we m
 ay refer you a recharge service\, upcoming funding/project calls\, and/or 
 other resources. We are unable to provide support for assigned coursework 
 (homework assignments\, class projects\, etc.) during office hours. If you
  need such help please contact your instructor or TA for assistance. If yo
 u need help with technical problems such system administration and hardwar
 e issues\, check out the the UC Davis ServiceHub and/or contact your depar
 tmental IT.
URL:https://events.library.ucdavis.edu/event/copy-of-collaborative-data-sc
 ience-office-hours-virtual
X-SOURCE:UC Davis Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:DataLab Speaker Series
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260602T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260602T113000
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52649350360565
DESCRIPTION:Join us for DataLab’s new speaker series this quarter! The s
 eries focuses on how researchers use data science methods and technologies
  in practice. It will include a mix of presentations about active and comp
 leted research projects\, industry projects\, new methods and technologies
  of interest to the community\, and more. The series is organized by DataL
 ab staff and graduate student affiliates.\n\n \n\n10:30-11:30 every Tuesda
 y in DataLab (Shields Library room 360). We will also stream on Zoom\, but
  for the best engagement we recomment attending in person if possible. Adv
 ance registration is required to receive the Zoom link.\n\nAll members of 
 the UC Davis community (students\, staff\, and faculty) are welcome to att
 end. Questions? Interested in presenting or becoming an affiliate? Contact
  datalab@ucdavis.edu.\n\n \n\nWe look forward to seeing you!
LOCATION:Shields Library\, 360
URL:https://events.library.ucdavis.edu/event/datalab-speaker-series-spring
 26
X-SOURCE:UC Davis Library
END:VEVENT
